<header>
  <div class="label">Market Insight · Alberta · January 2026</div>
  <h1>Alberta's January Price Spike Was Not Caused by Cold</h1>
  <p>During Alberta's coldest week of January 2026, wind — not temperature — determined when prices spiked.</p>
  <div class="meta">Data: AESO · Analysis: Independent · Published March 2026</div>
</header>

<div class="content">

  <div class="section">
    <h2>What happened</h2>
    <p>On January 4th, Alberta recorded temperatures as low as −31°C. The intuitive expectation — that extreme cold drives electricity prices up — did not hold. Prices remained relatively contained throughout the coldest period.</p>
    <p>The spike came the following morning, as temperatures began to recover. At 11am on January 5th, Alberta's pool price reached $858 CAD/MWh — the highest of the month. The difference between the two days was not temperature. It was wind.</p>

