    <div class="callout">
      When wind was high on January 4th, prices stayed low at −31°C. When wind dropped on January 5th, prices spiked to $858 — even as it got warmer. Temperature implied demand. Wind determined price.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section">
    <h2>What this shows</h2>
    <p>Cold weather increases electricity demand. But in Alberta's current generation mix — where wind supplies roughly 19% of output — wind availability has become a primary driver of price outcomes, sometimes outweighing demand signals entirely.</p>
    <p>This is not a failure of the system. It is a structural consequence of integrating variable renewables into an energy-only market. When wind runs, it suppresses prices. When it stops, gas peakers set the clearing price — and during periods of concurrent high demand, that price can be very high.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="closing-box">
    <h3>The broader point</h3>
    <p>The January 5th event illustrates a pattern that will become more common as Alberta's renewable capacity grows. Price outcomes increasingly reflect wind availability as much as demand conditions. A market designed around dispatchable generation — where demand largely determined price — faces new dynamics that a single uniform price cannot fully capture. This is part of what motivates Alberta's ongoing debate about market redesign.</p>
  </div>

